1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,034
At about six in the morning on the

2
00:00:02,054 --> 00:00:04,550
fifteenth of August, 1944,

3
00:00:04,570 --> 00:00:06,747
two military policemen on patrol at Fort

4
00:00:06,767 --> 00:00:09,258
Lawton, an Army post in Seattle above the

5
00:00:09,278 --> 00:00:11,768
water of Puget Sound, came to the obstacle

6
00:00:11,788 --> 00:00:13,965
course at the foot of a bluff.

7
00:00:13,985 --> 00:00:16,810
A man was hanging from one of its cables.

8
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:20,966
He was an Italian prisoner of war.

9
00:00:20,986 --> 00:00:24,780
His name was Guglielmo Olivotto.

10
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:29,300
His gravestone says he was born in October 1911.

11
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:30,813
He was thirty-two years old.

12
00:00:30,833 --> 00:00:33,537
A few hours earlier, the part of the fort

13
00:00:33,557 --> 00:00:36,280
above him had been the scene of a riot.

14
00:00:37,575 --> 00:00:39,809
Within three months, the Army put

15
00:00:39,829 --> 00:00:41,687
forty-three Black American soldiers on

16
00:00:41,707 --> 00:00:43,189
trial for that night.

17
00:00:43,209 --> 00:00:45,818
It was the largest and longest Army

18
00:00:45,838 --> 00:00:48,072
court-martial of the Second World War.

19
00:00:48,092 --> 00:00:50,701
The prosecutor would later become one of

20
00:00:50,721 --> 00:00:52,975
the most famous lawyers in America.

21
00:00:54,375 --> 00:00:56,624
This is the documented story of the night

22
00:00:56,644 --> 00:00:58,894
at Fort Lawton, of the report the defense

23
00:00:58,914 --> 00:01:00,312
was never allowed to read,

24
00:01:00,332 --> 00:01:02,582
and of the sixty-three years it took the

25
00:01:02,602 --> 00:01:05,155
Army to admit that the trial had been unfair.

26
00:01:06,875 --> 00:01:09,754
Fort Lawton sat on a bluff in the

27
00:01:09,774 --> 00:01:11,204
northwest corner of Seattle.

28
00:01:11,224 --> 00:01:14,103
In the war it became a port of

29
00:01:14,123 --> 00:01:16,640
embarkation, the last stop before the Pacific.

30
00:01:16,660 --> 00:01:19,715
A National Register report written in 1978

31
00:01:19,735 --> 00:01:20,818
put it plainly.

32
00:01:20,838 --> 00:01:23,390
At the height of World War II,

33
00:01:23,410 --> 00:01:25,595
over one million servicemen were processed

34
00:01:25,615 --> 00:01:27,963
thru Fort Lawton for the combat zones of

35
00:01:27,983 --> 00:01:29,755
the Pacific and the Far East.

36
00:01:31,125 --> 00:01:33,535
The Italians were there because their

37
00:01:33,555 --> 00:01:35,155
country had changed sides.

38
00:01:35,175 --> 00:01:39,093
In October 1943, Italy declared war on

39
00:01:39,113 --> 00:01:41,872
Germany, and the Army asked its Italian

40
00:01:41,892 --> 00:01:44,253
prisoners in America to volunteer for

41
00:01:44,273 --> 00:01:45,047
service units.

42
00:01:45,067 --> 00:01:47,032
Of about fifty-one thousand Italian

43
00:01:47,052 --> 00:01:49,414
prisoners in the country, more than

44
00:01:49,434 --> 00:01:50,625
forty-five thousand joined.

45
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:53,258
They were still prisoners,

46
00:01:53,278 --> 00:01:55,250
but they worked for the Army.

47
00:01:55,270 --> 00:01:57,574
Each unit had between forty and two

48
00:01:57,594 --> 00:02:00,230
hundred and fifty men, under an Italian officer.

49
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:02,222
They were paid twenty-four dollars a

50
00:02:02,242 --> 00:02:05,230
month, and only a third of it in cash.

51
00:02:06,550 --> 00:02:08,888
At Fort Lawton, the twenty-eighth Italian

52
00:02:08,908 --> 00:02:11,247
Quartermaster Service Unit had about two

53
00:02:11,267 --> 00:02:12,819
hundred and six men.

54
00:02:12,839 --> 00:02:15,590
On some evenings, they went into town.

55
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:20,233
Next to them lived about six hundred and

56
00:02:20,253 --> 00:02:21,709
twelve Black American soldiers,

57
00:02:21,729 --> 00:02:23,185
in three port companies.

58
00:02:23,205 --> 00:02:24,662
The Army was segregated.

59
00:02:24,682 --> 00:02:27,245
At Fort Lawton, the Black soldiers were

60
00:02:27,265 --> 00:02:30,198
housed in their own remote part of the

61
00:02:30,218 --> 00:02:32,782
fort, right beside the barracks of the

62
00:02:32,802 --> 00:02:33,602
Italian prisoners.

63
00:02:34,950 --> 00:02:37,374
On the night of the fourteenth of August,

64
00:02:37,394 --> 00:02:39,512
the port companies were in the final

65
00:02:39,532 --> 00:02:40,734
preparation for shipment overseas,

66
00:02:40,754 --> 00:02:41,670
the next morning.

67
00:02:42,975 --> 00:02:46,315
The Black soldiers served in a segregated Army.

68
00:02:46,335 --> 00:02:49,255
The men next door were enemy prisoners,

69
00:02:49,275 --> 00:02:50,935
with passes into town.

70
00:02:50,955 --> 00:02:53,455
Newspapers at the time blamed resentment

71
00:02:53,475 --> 00:02:54,735
of those privileges.

72
00:02:56,525 --> 00:02:58,332
At about eleven that night,

73
00:02:58,352 --> 00:03:00,889
four Black soldiers were walking back to

74
00:03:00,909 --> 00:03:01,620
their barracks.

75
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:03,812
They met a group of Italians.

76
00:03:03,832 --> 00:03:06,370
The account the Army later accepted says

77
00:03:06,390 --> 00:03:09,293
one of the Americans swore at them and

78
00:03:09,313 --> 00:03:10,389
shouted, hey, Italian.

79
00:03:10,409 --> 00:03:11,851
An Italian turned around.

80
00:03:11,871 --> 00:03:14,408
An American walked toward him with what

81
00:03:14,428 --> 00:03:16,235
appeared to be a knife.

82
00:03:16,255 --> 00:03:19,158
The Italian hit him with his fist and

83
00:03:19,178 --> 00:03:21,005
knocked him to the ground.

84
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:23,926
Accounts differ on who started it,

85
00:03:23,946 --> 00:03:26,120
but every account agrees on the punch that

86
00:03:26,140 --> 00:03:28,060
put a Black soldier on the ground.

87
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:32,175
What came next was not a fistfight.

88
00:03:32,195 --> 00:03:34,970
Between one hundred and two hundred men

89
00:03:34,990 --> 00:03:36,966
went into the Italian area.

90
00:03:36,986 --> 00:03:39,761
The Army's own summary says they carried

91
00:03:39,781 --> 00:03:41,359
stones, knives, shovels, clubs,

92
00:03:41,379 --> 00:03:43,375
and at least one ax.

93
00:03:44,075 --> 00:03:46,871
Some of the military police were slow to

94
00:03:46,891 --> 00:03:47,575
step in.

95
00:03:47,595 --> 00:03:50,391
They took the injured Black soldier to the

96
00:03:50,411 --> 00:03:52,151
hospital farthest from the fight.

97
00:03:52,171 --> 00:03:55,319
The riot went on for about an hour before

98
00:03:55,339 --> 00:03:57,431
the military police broke it up.

99
00:03:57,451 --> 00:03:59,915
Twenty-four men were taken to the hospital.

100
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:04,300
At six the next morning, the two military

101
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:06,902
policemen found Guglielmo Olivotto on the

102
00:04:06,922 --> 00:04:08,649
obstacle course, about four hundred feet

103
00:04:08,669 --> 00:04:11,580
down a steep path from the scene of the riot.

104
00:04:12,925 --> 00:04:16,037
The doctor who examined the body found no

105
00:04:16,057 --> 00:04:17,211
bruises on it.

106
00:04:17,231 --> 00:04:20,735
The time of death was put at about midnight.

107
00:04:20,755 --> 00:04:23,475
Nobody photographed the body while it was

108
00:04:23,495 --> 00:04:24,258
still hanging.

109
00:04:24,278 --> 00:04:25,432
Nobody took fingerprints.

110
00:04:25,452 --> 00:04:28,173
There were two footprints under the body.

111
00:04:28,193 --> 00:04:30,913
The provost marshal put a piece of

112
00:04:30,933 --> 00:04:34,436
cardboard over them, and no cast was ever made.

113
00:04:34,456 --> 00:04:36,805
Later the rope itself was lost.

114
00:04:38,525 --> 00:04:40,534
By the fall, forty-three Black soldiers

115
00:04:40,554 --> 00:04:42,901
from the port companies were charged with

116
00:04:42,921 --> 00:04:45,268
rioting, which carried a maximum of life

117
00:04:45,288 --> 00:04:45,944
in prison.

118
00:04:45,964 --> 00:04:48,649
Three of them were also charged with the

119
00:04:48,669 --> 00:04:51,545
murder of Guglielmo Olivotto.

120
00:04:51,565 --> 00:04:53,945
That carried a maximum of death.

121
00:04:55,250 --> 00:04:58,257
At the same time, the Army sent a general

122
00:04:58,277 --> 00:05:00,274
from the Inspector General's office to

123
00:05:00,294 --> 00:05:03,301
find out what had gone wrong at the fort.

124
00:05:03,321 --> 00:05:05,426
Brigadier General Elliot Cooke took sworn

125
00:05:05,446 --> 00:05:07,992
testimony from one hundred and sixty-four

126
00:05:08,012 --> 00:05:08,420
witnesses.

127
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:10,987
His report was finished on the

128
00:05:11,007 --> 00:05:13,490
twenty-eighth of October, 1944.

129
00:05:13,510 --> 00:05:17,570
He called the fort's investigation reprehensible.

130
00:05:18,975 --> 00:05:20,602
The report had consequences,

131
00:05:20,622 --> 00:05:22,661
but not for the trial.

132
00:05:22,681 --> 00:05:24,720
The fort's commanding officer was

133
00:05:24,740 --> 00:05:27,190
relieved, and two white military policemen

134
00:05:27,210 --> 00:05:30,484
who had failed to step in were court-martialed.

135
00:05:30,504 --> 00:05:32,975
The report itself was stamped confidential.

136
00:05:34,850 --> 00:05:36,930
The trial began on the sixteenth of

137
00:05:36,950 --> 00:05:39,730
November, 1944.

138
00:05:39,750 --> 00:05:42,348
The prosecutor was a lawyer from Texas,

139
00:05:42,368 --> 00:05:44,850
Lieutenant Colonel Leon Jaworski.

140
00:05:45,550 --> 00:05:47,910
The defense was a Seattle lawyer in

141
00:05:47,930 --> 00:05:49,550
uniform, Major William Beeks,

142
00:05:49,570 --> 00:05:50,497
with one assistant.

143
00:05:50,517 --> 00:05:52,708
In the words a congressman later wrote

144
00:05:52,728 --> 00:05:55,234
into a bill, the defendants were tried in

145
00:05:55,254 --> 00:05:57,445
a group, and all forty-three shared two

146
00:05:57,465 --> 00:05:59,655
Army defense counsel, who were given just

147
00:05:59,675 --> 00:06:01,570
ten days to prepare the defense.

148
00:06:02,925 --> 00:06:05,808
Jaworski had the Cooke report all through

149
00:06:05,828 --> 00:06:06,662
the trial.

150
00:06:06,682 --> 00:06:09,485
In open court, Beeks asked to see it.

151
00:06:09,505 --> 00:06:12,888
Jaworski refused, and the officers on the

152
00:06:12,908 --> 00:06:14,574
panel did not step in.

153
00:06:14,594 --> 00:06:16,934
The prosecution was still allowed to use

154
00:06:16,954 --> 00:06:17,965
statements from it.

155
00:06:19,100 --> 00:06:21,716
The central witness for the prosecution

156
00:06:21,736 --> 00:06:23,474
was an Italian prisoner.

157
00:06:23,494 --> 00:06:26,549
Army intelligence had already looked at him.

158
00:06:26,569 --> 00:06:29,186
The congressman's bill says it plainly:

159
00:06:29,206 --> 00:06:30,943
Army intelligence had previously

160
00:06:30,963 --> 00:06:33,140
identified him as probably pro-Nazi,

161
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:36,675
a fact not made available to the defense.

162
00:06:37,375 --> 00:06:39,237
There was no murder weapon.

163
00:06:39,257 --> 00:06:41,873
No witness and no evidence tied any

164
00:06:41,893 --> 00:06:45,850
defendant to the death of Guglielmo Olivotto.

165
00:06:46,950 --> 00:06:48,310
On the eighteenth of December,

166
00:06:48,330 --> 00:06:51,817
1944, the court gave its verdicts.

167
00:06:51,837 --> 00:06:54,850
Twenty-eight men were convicted of rioting.

168
00:06:54,870 --> 00:06:58,894
Two of them were also convicted of manslaughter.

169
00:06:58,914 --> 00:07:00,430
Thirteen were acquitted.

170
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:04,430
The sentences ran from six months to

171
00:07:04,450 --> 00:07:05,187
twenty-five years.

172
00:07:05,207 --> 00:07:08,593
All but one of the men who were convicted

173
00:07:08,613 --> 00:07:10,107
got a dishonorable discharge.

174
00:07:10,127 --> 00:07:12,000
On the nineteenth of April,

175
00:07:12,020 --> 00:07:15,333
1945, an Army review board looked at one

176
00:07:15,353 --> 00:07:17,886
of the biggest trial records of the war,

177
00:07:17,906 --> 00:07:19,820
and dismissed every appeal without comment.

178
00:07:21,100 --> 00:07:22,591
The men went to prison.

179
00:07:22,611 --> 00:07:25,613
By the end of the decade, the last of them

180
00:07:25,633 --> 00:07:27,427
was out, carrying a dishonorable discharge

181
00:07:27,447 --> 00:07:29,260
for the rest of his life.

182
00:07:30,950 --> 00:07:33,321
After the war, the prosecutor tried German

183
00:07:33,341 --> 00:07:36,053
war criminals, and then became one of the

184
00:07:36,073 --> 00:07:38,102
best known lawyers in the country.

185
00:07:38,122 --> 00:07:41,710
On the first of November, 1973,

186
00:07:41,730 --> 00:07:43,622
Leon Jaworski became the special

187
00:07:43,642 --> 00:07:45,410
prosecutor in the Watergate scandal.

188
00:07:45,430 --> 00:07:48,270
He took the President's tapes all the way

189
00:07:48,290 --> 00:07:49,700
to the Supreme Court.

190
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:52,560
Fort Lawton was not part of the story

191
00:07:52,580 --> 00:07:54,010
people told about him.

192
00:07:55,650 --> 00:07:58,888
Guglielmo Olivotto was buried in the small

193
00:07:58,908 --> 00:08:00,606
post cemetery at Fort Lawton,

194
00:08:00,626 --> 00:08:03,375
in an area away from the American graves.

195
00:08:04,075 --> 00:08:07,473
In 1986, a Seattle television reporter

196
00:08:07,493 --> 00:08:09,959
named Jack Hamann found his headstone.

197
00:08:09,979 --> 00:08:12,860
Hamann made a documentary about the riot.

198
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:16,195
At the time, he believed the Army's version.

199
00:08:17,525 --> 00:08:19,985
Years later, Hamann and his wife Leslie

200
00:08:20,005 --> 00:08:21,756
went back to the case.

201
00:08:21,776 --> 00:08:23,882
At the National Archives in Maryland,

202
00:08:23,902 --> 00:08:25,451
Leslie found the Cooke report,

203
00:08:25,471 --> 00:08:27,381
declassified, in a miscellaneous section

204
00:08:27,401 --> 00:08:28,945
of the war records.

205
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:32,944
In 2005, the Hamanns published a book

206
00:08:32,964 --> 00:08:35,228
about the case, On American Soil.

207
00:08:35,248 --> 00:08:37,131
That summer, Seattle congressman Jim

208
00:08:37,151 --> 00:08:39,414
McDermott put a bill before Congress,

209
00:08:39,434 --> 00:08:42,459
and its findings told the story the trial

210
00:08:42,479 --> 00:08:43,279
had not.

211
00:08:44,925 --> 00:08:48,327
The families of the convicted men had to

212
00:08:48,347 --> 00:08:50,038
apply one by one.

213
00:08:50,058 --> 00:08:53,032
In October 2007, the Army Board for

214
00:08:53,052 --> 00:08:56,026
Correction of Military Records gave its answer.

215
00:08:56,046 --> 00:08:58,185
Its three members voted unanimously.

216
00:08:59,625 --> 00:09:02,255
The board wrote that it is unimaginable

217
00:09:02,275 --> 00:09:04,906
that two attorneys could fully and freely

218
00:09:04,926 --> 00:09:06,042
represent each accused.

219
00:09:06,062 --> 00:09:08,692
It called the prosecutor's use of the

220
00:09:08,712 --> 00:09:11,721
report the most egregious error in the trial.

221
00:09:11,741 --> 00:09:14,750
Its conclusion was that the trial had been

222
00:09:14,770 --> 00:09:16,285
fundamentally unfair and improper.

223
00:09:17,650 --> 00:09:20,190
The board did not say who killed Guglielmo

224
00:09:20,210 --> 00:09:23,151
Olivotto, and it did not rule on guilt.

225
00:09:23,171 --> 00:09:24,666
It set aside the convictions,

226
00:09:24,686 --> 00:09:25,877
made the discharges honorable,

227
00:09:25,897 --> 00:09:28,604
and ordered the Army to pay what the men

228
00:09:28,624 --> 00:09:29,424
had lost.

229
00:09:30,975 --> 00:09:33,120
Samuel Snow, of Leesburg, Florida,

230
00:09:33,140 --> 00:09:35,286
was one of the twenty-eight.

231
00:09:35,306 --> 00:09:39,183
After prison, he took a job as a janitor.

232
00:09:39,203 --> 00:09:42,235
In November 2007, his back pay arrived.

233
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:46,791
On the floor of the United States Senate,

234
00:09:46,811 --> 00:09:49,689
Senator Bill Nelson of Florida asked the

235
00:09:49,709 --> 00:09:50,930
question for him.

236
00:09:50,950 --> 00:09:51,344
Mr.

237
00:09:51,364 --> 00:09:54,656
President, do you know how much that was?

238
00:09:54,676 --> 00:09:57,139
It was seven hundred and twenty-five

239
00:09:57,159 --> 00:09:59,225
dollars, 1944 dollars.

240
00:09:59,925 --> 00:10:01,585
There was no interest.

241
00:10:01,605 --> 00:10:04,945
Congress added it by law in October 2008.

242
00:10:04,965 --> 00:10:08,745
By then, it was too late for Samuel Snow.

243
00:10:10,475 --> 00:10:12,725
On Saturday, the twenty-sixth of July,

244
00:10:12,745 --> 00:10:15,751
2008, the Army held a ceremony at Fort

245
00:10:15,771 --> 00:10:18,399
Lawton, which was now a city park.

246
00:10:18,419 --> 00:10:20,669
An assistant secretary of the Army,

247
00:10:20,689 --> 00:10:22,939
Ronald James, stood before the families.

248
00:10:22,959 --> 00:10:25,965
He told them the Army was genuinely sorry,

249
00:10:25,985 --> 00:10:28,255
and that he was genuinely sorry.

250
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:32,362
A few days before, a congressman from

251
00:10:32,382 --> 00:10:35,541
Washington had told the House what that meant.

252
00:10:35,561 --> 00:10:37,925
Only two of the original twenty-eight

253
00:10:37,945 --> 00:10:39,912
soldiers are alive today, meaning

254
00:10:39,932 --> 00:10:41,898
twenty-six Army veterans passed away

255
00:10:41,918 --> 00:10:44,283
before an extreme personal injustice was

256
00:10:44,303 --> 00:10:45,103
rectified.

257
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,683
Samuel Snow had come to Seattle for it.

258
00:10:48,703 --> 00:10:49,696
He was eighty-four.

259
00:10:49,716 --> 00:10:51,723
At the dinner the night before,

260
00:10:51,743 --> 00:10:55,101
he felt ill, and he was taken to the hospital.

261
00:10:55,121 --> 00:10:57,804
His son went to the ceremony and accepted

262
00:10:57,824 --> 00:10:59,493
the honorable discharge for him,

263
00:10:59,513 --> 00:11:01,540
and brought it back to him.

264
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:04,255
According to Senator Nelson,

265
00:11:04,275 --> 00:11:06,468
he clutched it in his hands.

266
00:11:06,488 --> 00:11:08,700
A few hours later, he died.

267
00:11:10,425 --> 00:11:12,580
Today, nobody stands convicted of the

268
00:11:12,600 --> 00:11:15,625
death of Guglielmo Olivotto.

269
00:11:15,645 --> 00:11:17,925
The two manslaughter convictions were set

270
00:11:17,945 --> 00:11:19,458
aside with the rest.

271
00:11:19,478 --> 00:11:22,140
Jack Hamann points to a white military

272
00:11:22,160 --> 00:11:24,823
policeman who was present at every key

273
00:11:24,843 --> 00:11:27,889
moment in the forty-eight hours around his death.

274
